WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.322 --> 00:00:04.702
This throwback sitcom sequel is keeping one&nbsp;
controversial element from the original.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.702 --> 00:00:08.280
To some fans, there's nothing funny&nbsp;
about bringing back this sitcom trope.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:14.100
Originally created in the 1940s, the laugh track&nbsp;
was first made to help bring an artificial "at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.960
the theater" experience for radio listeners at&nbsp;
home. When TV began to take off in the '50s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.660
it was used to enhance the actual laughs that came&nbsp;
from a live studio audience. And it worked, too. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.460
study from the University College London confirmed&nbsp;
that hearing background laughter, such as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.210
chuckles from a pre-recorded laugh track, trick&nbsp;
the brain into thinking bad jokes are funnier.

00:00:34.277 --> 00:00:38.255
"Haha, that's actually hilarious."

00:00:38.255 --> 00:00:42.120
However, towards the end of the&nbsp;
'90s/early 2000s, popular shows like&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.160
"The Larry Sanders Show," "Malcolm in the Middle",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.180
"Arrested Development" and others proved&nbsp;
that fans no longer needed their hands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.260
held when it came to knowing when to laugh.&nbsp;
Now, in the current television landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.180
laugh tracks can come off as antiquated. While&nbsp;
the artistic choice isn't as popular these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.740
Netflix's new "That '90s Show" series brings&nbsp;
the laugh track back with a vengeance.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.640
The streaming giant recently debuted&nbsp;
the 10-episode first season of "That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.900
'90s Show." For those who don't know, it's&nbsp;
linked to the original "That '70s Show,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.860
with many of the same characters/actors&nbsp;
popping up in this latest installment.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:19.080
However, returning actors aren't the only holdover&nbsp;
that fans might recognize from the original. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.580
laugh track is back, and based on comments&nbsp;
on social media following the show's release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.729
some viewers are not happy with&nbsp;
this artificial audience reaction.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:33.660
"It's a normal temperature, Eric. You've&nbsp;
just got that sensitive baby skin."

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:40.020
Whether it was to enhance live audience reactions&nbsp;
due to poor microphones back in the day or to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:44.880
replace audiences altogether, the laugh track&nbsp;
has been around for a long time. With plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.960
of early '90s sitcoms continuing the trend,&nbsp;
it seems like it would be a smart choice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.580
have Netflix's new series, "That '90s Show,"&nbsp;
use the same background enhancement. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.840
it's clear that there are plenty of viewers&nbsp;
out there who no longer accept this tactic.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.260
One Twitter user didn't seem&nbsp;
happy with it at all, commenting,

00:02:08.339 --> 00:02:09.360
Another declared,

00:02:13.402 --> 00:02:18.180
Viewers over on Reddit were also not too&nbsp;
fond of this throwback tactic. One wrote,

00:02:21.143 --> 00:02:22.020
Another wrote,

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:31.260
"I don't know how the whole puberty&nbsp;
thing is going for you. Probably great."

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.980
Despite the negativity, there&nbsp;
were some users who totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.220
understood the reasoning for using&nbsp;
the audio, with commenter writing,

00:02:43.031 --> 00:02:45.960
Other users echoed a similar&nbsp;
sentiment, with one saying,

00:02:55.294 --> 00:02:57.480
As it turns out, just like its predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.200
"That '90s Show" was shot in front of&nbsp;
a live studio audience in Hollywood.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.060
While the laughs are authentic, so is&nbsp;
the use of a laugh track. Laugh tracks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.620
continued to be popular among viewers until&nbsp;
the 2000s. With the sitcom set in the '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.220
it would arguably be inauthentic&nbsp;
to debut the series without some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.060
background chuckling. At least, that's&nbsp;
what some fans think. One fan offered,

00:03:22.847 --> 00:03:23.627
"Really?"

00:03:23.627 --> 00:03:28.620
"That '90s Show" isn't the only modern legacy&nbsp;
sequel/reboot that utilizes a laugh track.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.240
Consider Hulu's "How I Met Your Father," the&nbsp;
spinoff sequel series to CBS' fan-favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.320
"How I Met Your Mother." Just like "That '90s&nbsp;
Show," "How I Met Your Father" was criticized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.660
for utilizing a laugh track, with many dubbing&nbsp;
its use corny and antiquated. For "How I Met Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:47.100
Father" co-showrunner Isaac Aptaker, however,&nbsp;
bringing the laugh track back was necessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:51.900
as it continued the same vibe as the original.&nbsp;
The same can be said for "That '90s Show."

